  "slug": "untitled-topic-af7b",
  "summary": "Five new models link network topology, movement and social rewiring to host-parasite evolution, herbivore impacts and phenology under environmental change.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that network or spatial structure mediates how individual-level traits and behaviours scale to population, evolutionary and ecosystem outcomes. Simulations on 268 empirical social networks indicate that compensatory tie formation alters predicted consequences of individual loss. Host-parasite models show that degree heterogeneity in spatial networks shapes host resistance and spatial variation in parasite infectivity, and that parasite-impaired host mobility favours reduced virulence via relatedness and host density. A spatially explicit model links herbivore movement and landscape structure to resource depletion and population dynamics, and an individual-based model shows that social information use synchronises breeding but constrains tracking of rapid environmental change. What is new is the explicit coupling of movement, rewiring or information use to evolutionary and demographic responses. All items are simulation or theory based, so conclusions remain model-dependent.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
